        <briefdescription>
<para>Safely parse a numeric id query parameter, replying 400 on failure. </para>
        </briefdescription>
        <detaileddescription>
<para>Calling std::stoll directly on req-&gt;getParameter(name) would throw std::invalid_argument or std::out_of_range while building the handler&apos;s argument list, i.e. before executeControllerMethod&apos;s try/catch is entered; the exception then escaped Drogon&apos;s dispatch as a raw, empty-body 500. This helper contains the throw and emits a normal JSON 400 instead. On failure the callback has already been invoked with the error response. <parameterlist kind="param"><parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername direction="in">req</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>Request whose query parameter is read. </para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
<parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername direction="in">name</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>Parameter name to parse as an integer id. </para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
<parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername direction="inout">callback</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>Response callback; invoked with a 400 when parsing fails. </para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
</parameterlist>
<simplesect kind="return"><para>The parsed id, or std::nullopt on failure (callback already answered). </para>
</simplesect>
<simplesect kind="post"><para>If std::nullopt is returned the caller must return early without calling executeControllerMethod, otherwise a second response would be sent. </para>
</simplesect>
</para>
        </detaileddescription>
