        <briefdescription>
<para>Dispatch a controller method for a keyed entity type, off the IO thread when needed. </para>
        </briefdescription>
        <detaileddescription>
<para>Looks up <computeroutput>key</computeroutput> in <computeroutput>registry</computeroutput> (an unordered_map of std::variant controller pointers); a missing key is answered with 400 and no controller is invoked. A model callback (int status + content) is adapted to HTTP responses: OK -&gt; success, INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR -&gt; failure (500 body), anything else -&gt; error with that numeric status. <ref refid="structRequester" kindref="compound">Requester</ref> identity is read from the &quot;clientID&quot;/&quot;clientGroup&quot; attributes that the Auth filter inserts on SECURE routes (INSECURE routes such as client create/login also use this helper). When executor.ioFastPath() is enabled the call is first tried on the IO thread inside a <ref refid="classDbProbe" kindref="compound">DbProbe</ref>: work that needs no connection completes there, whereas the first <ref refid="structWouldBlock" kindref="compound">WouldBlock</ref> is caught and the whole call is re-queued on the DB executor from the start. Request, arguments and callback are captured by value because queued work finishes after this function returns on a worker thread. A full executor queue is answered 503 without running the controller; <ref refid="classDatabaseBusy" kindref="compound">DatabaseBusy</ref> becomes 503, and any other std::exception becomes a 500 failure response. <parameterlist kind="templateparam"><parameteritem>
